0 ~Batima block 2 x 2 - hollow studs, conical sockets, cast stone
0 Name: batima-2x2.dat
0 Author: spex project (geometry read off photographs of the real object)
0 !LDRAW_ORG Unofficial_Part
0 !LICENSE Licensed under CC BY 4.0 : see CAreadme.txt
0 // MODELLED FROM PHOTOGRAPHS OF REAL BATIMA BLOCKS, not from a patent
0 // drawing and not from a modern brick. Stefan photographed a set with its
0 // albums: "BATIMA - Le Jeu de Construction ideal - ALBUM des Numeros
0 // 1-2-3-4-5-6-7 (le numero 6 = 750 blocs; le numero 7 = 900 blocs) -
0 // Brevete", with Dutch text on the same sheets, so Belgian and French/Dutch
0 // - consistent with Le Batima Soc. and FR 588985, c. 1924.
0 //
0 // WHAT THE PHOTOGRAPHS SHOW, and why the previous placeholder was wrong in
0 // every particular:
0 //
0 //   THE STUDS ARE HOLLOW. Every stud is a raised RING with a bore straight
0 //   down its middle. A LEGO stud is a solid cylinder. This is the single
0 //   most visible difference and it is on every block in every photograph.
0 //
0 //   THE UNDERSIDE IS A FIELD OF CONICAL SOCKETS, one per stud position -
0 //   countersunk cups, each with a small hole at the bottom. There is no
0 //   tube, no wall clutch, and no hollow shell: the coupling is a cone
0 //   entering a cone. It is a different mechanism from 1940 and 1949, not a
0 //   worse version of the same one.
0 //
0 //   IT IS CAST STONE, not plastic. Cream and terracotta, speckled and
0 //   weathered. `batima.ldr` binds 503 Very_Light_Grey for the cream, which
0 //   is dE 10.1 from the photographed colour; 422 Sienna_Brown (dE 7.7) is
0 //   the terracotta if the shot ever wants it.
0 //
0 // PROPORTIONS ARE READ OFF THE PHOTOGRAPHS AND ARE NOT MEASUREMENTS. The
0 // pitch is kept at LDraw's 20 LDU so this block stands beside the other two
0 // at a comparable size, which is what A3-S02 and A3-S03 compare. The height
0 // is 20 LDU rather than a brick's 24, because the real block reads flatter
0 // than a LEGO brick against its own width. Four numbers would replace every
0 // estimate here and none of them needs more than a caliper: stud pitch,
0 // block height, stud outer diameter, bore diameter.
0 //
0 // NO `BFC CERTIFY`: the body quads are hand-wound and an unverifiable
0 // certification is worse than none.
0 //
0 // LDraw primitives used under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence.
0 // LDraw is a trademark of the LDraw Organization.

0 // ---- body: a plain block, 40 x 40 LDU footprint, 20 LDU tall.
0 //      LDraw +Y down, so the top face is y = 0 and the bottom is y = 20.
4 16 -20 0 -20 20 0 -20 20 0 20 -20 0 20
4 16 -20 20 -20 20 20 -20 20 20 20 -20 20 20
4 16 -20 20 -20 20 20 -20 20 0 -20 -20 0 -20
4 16 20 20 20 -20 20 20 -20 0 20 20 0 20
4 16 20 20 -20 20 20 20 20 0 20 20 0 -20
4 16 -20 20 20 -20 20 -20 -20 0 -20 -20 0 20

0 // ---- four hollow studs, 5 LDU tall, outer radius 5, bore radius 2.
0 //      Outer wall, bore wall, and the annulus that closes the top between
0 //      them (ring2 scaled 2 spans 4..6, ring1 scaled 2 spans 2..4).
1 16 -10 0 -10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-stud.dat
1 16 10 0 -10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-stud.dat
1 16 -10 0 10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-stud.dat
1 16 10 0 10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-stud.dat

0 // ---- four conical sockets in the underside, aligned with the studs above.
1 16 -10 20 -10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-socket.dat
1 16 10 20 -10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-socket.dat
1 16 -10 20 10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-socket.dat
1 16 10 20 10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-socket.dat
